      },
      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Nanoparticulate systems are new tools that promise a revolution in the field of drug delivery. Among their numerous benefits, polyelectrolyte complexes (PECs) have shown to provide a barrier to drug release."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "In this study, PECs, in the form of self-assembled polymeric nanogels, have been studied as potential drug carriers of the freely soluble drug tramadol HCL trying to achieve a prolonged percutaneous permeation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODOLOGY",
          "content": "The hydrogels were subjected to swelling, rheology, release and permeation studies and were characterized by FTIR spectroscopy and scanning electron microscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "P2 hydrogel composed of chitosan-carrageenan (1-1) PEC attained the most compromised rheological shear-thinning thixotropic behavior, good bioadhesive properties, the most retarded release and permeation with an f2 value <50 compared to chitosan hydrogel, altogether with non-irritancy to the skin. SEM photographs showed that P2 has spherical nanosized particles structure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This approach can provide us promising results for an around-the-clock analgesia with better safety and tolerability profile."
        }
      ]},
